  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nBy Friday, upper level ridging begins to establish across the \nregion, ushering in dry weather this weekend lasting into early next \nweek. While this ridge tracks across the region, will see \ntemperatures steadily climbing this weekend, though highs are not \nexpected to get as warm as the previous two weekends. In fact, \ncurrent forecast highs sit around the 75th percentile compared to \nlocal climatology through Sunday, only reaching the 90th percentile \nby Monday. For reference, our typical highs late July into early \nAugust actually begin to cool slightly dropping from 90 to 89. \nForecast highs this weekend are mostly in the upper 80s to mid 90s, \nthough some portions of the Panhandle may break into the upper 90s. \nWhile we warm through the day, do expect that lows will dip into the \n60s overnight each night, which should provide at least some relief. \nThe only real concern for heat in the long term appears to be \nMonday, when we may see highs climbing into the mid to upper 90s \nacross most of the region, which as previously mentioned is around \nthe 90th percentile of our local climatology. Even then, expecting \nthis will remain well below record values (which are around 105). \n\nAs the heat slowly builds this weekend and weather remains dry, will \nlikely see increasing fire weather concerns once again, especially \nSunday and Monday. With the increasing temperatures, may see \nafternoon humidity values dropping around 20 to 25 percent across \nthe Panhandle into the Sandhills, with some potential to see these \ndrop lower. Sunday and Monday in particular, winds may become quite \ngusty across the Sandhills, with peak gusts out of the south around \n30 to 40 mph. Given that little additional precipitation is expected \nin the next 48 hours across the Sandhills and that we will be \nwarming again, these winds provide enough concern for at least \nelevated to near critical fire weather conditions. Will continue to \nkeep a close eye on forecast trends as we head into the weekend. \n\nMonday afternoon, the ridge begins to break down as an upper level \ntrough begins to track into the region (largely driving the wind and \nfire concerns Monday). This should help to push a cold front across \nthe region Monday night into Tuesday morning. As this slightly more \nunsettled pattern arrives, should see a return to more seasonal \ntemperatures, with highs expected to return to the upper 80s. This \nwill also bring better chances of precipitation, however, ensemble \nguidance remains in disagreement on this. The European ensembles \nsuggest a higher chance of rain, around 50 percent or greater of \nexceeding one tenth of an inch Tuesday into Wednesday. Meanwhile, \nthe GEFS only shows a 30 percent chance of this happening. Both seem \nin agreement for measurable rainfall, but given the uncertainty on \nexceeding one tenth, expect this will likely be a low end rain \nevent, if anything occurs.",
